TH=[
("reason","Reason, doubt & knowledge","Critical thinking, rationality, scientific temper, the value of questions and opposing views.","Build around the tension between certainty and doubt; use a scientist, a judge and a citizen as three lenses."),
("journey","Life as a journey: adversity & resilience","Perseverance, failure as teacher, change, risk-taking and the meaning of the journey over the destination.","Open with a personal-scale story, widen to society and nation; end with what the journey changes in us."),
("inner","Happiness, contentment & the inner self","Gratitude, simplicity, mindfulness, paradoxes of life and how we see ourselves and others.","Avoid preaching: balance inner contentment with ambition; draw on Indian philosophy and modern psychology."),
("ethics","Choices, ethics & the courage to act","Hard decisions, action versus inaction, truth, prudence and values.","Use dilemmas: show the cost of inaction with a concrete public example, then the ethical principle."),
("power","Leadership, power & conflict","What power reveals, servant leadership, conflict, war and its victims, statecraft.","Pair a historical leader with a contemporary institution; show power's effect on the 'grass' (ordinary people)."),
("nature","Nature, environment & civilisation","Forests, ecology, nature as spirit and as economy, sustainability.","Move from ecology to economy to ethics; India's traditions (sacred groves) give strong examples."),
("justice","Society, gender & justice","Patriarchy, gender roles, justice versus charity, equity.","Use data plus a human story; contrast justice (rights, structures) with charity (benevolence)."),
("learning","Education, creativity & imagination","What education means, curiosity, creativity, the power of ideas and the 'empire of the mind'.","Show how ideas travel from a classroom or lab into society; cite one Indian and one global innovator."),
("tech","Technology, media & the modern mind","Social media, self-discovery, technology and power, AI.","Keep it human: technology as a mirror of choices, not destiny; balance benefits and harms."),
("history","Time, history & change","Learning from history, constant change, culture and civilisation.","Use the arc of time: past lesson, present parallel, future warning."),
("policy","India's development & governance","Direct policy-style debates: farming, federalism, growth, tourism.","Mostly before 2020; useful as a source of examples for the philosophical topics now asked."),
]
notes=[
 ("Philosophy has replaced policy","From 2013 to 2017, many topics were direct policy debates (federalism, tourism, farming, jobless growth). Since 2020, all 56 topics are aphorisms or abstract ideas. Both sections now test how you think, not what you know."),
 ("Five clusters dominate since 2020","Life as a journey and resilience; reason, doubt and knowledge; happiness and the inner self; choices, ethics and action; and leadership and power. Together they account for most topics. Nature (forests appear in 2022, 2024 and 2026) and gender justice are steady side-streams."),
 ("Paradox is the house style","'A thorn is a changed bud', 'Oxymorons reflect the ironies of life', 'The real is rational', 'A good leader follows the followers': most topics hide a contradiction. A thesis, then antithesis, then synthesis structure suits them well."),
 ("Power and conflict are rising","2024's 'test character by giving power', 2025's 'subdue the enemy without fighting', and 2026's 'elephants fight, grass gets trampled' and 'leader follows the followers' suggest leadership and conflict will keep appearing, given wars in West Asia and Europe."),
 ("Your raw material is the newspaper","Philosophical topics are won with concrete stories: a farmer, a judge, a sportsperson, a scientist. Each day's paper gives one or two of these. The Essay Column below files them under these themes so you have a ready bank by exam time."),
 ("A simple bank per theme","For each theme, aim to collect two stories (one Indian, one global), two short quotes, two data points, one historical example and one book reference. Rotate the dimensions (individual, society, nation, world, future) in the body."),
]
F=[ # fodder items from IE 27 Oct 2026: (type,title,text,use,themes,src,link-topics)
("Story","Tigress T-138: not all who wander are lost","Airlifted from Bihar to West Bengal's Buxa reserve to restart a tiger population, T-138 walked 35 km into Assam's Raimona National Park. Forest officials chose to let her explore, saying it is natural for tigers to range 70-80 km before settling.","An opening anecdote for 'Not all who wander are lost': wandering as exploration, not failure. Also nature and the futility of drawing borders around life.",["journey","nature"],"IE p. 11",["2023A3","2026B1"]),
("Story","Jogen Chowdhury: refugee to master painter","Born into a landed family in Faridpur in pre-Partition East Bengal, he suffered a great reversal of fortune and became a post-Partition refugee in Calcutta. Drawing on memories of the kantha embroidery and alpona floor art of Bengal's village women, he built a personal modern idiom. The tribute calls him a 'hard optimist' and a 'dreamer of institutions'.","For 'A thorn is a changed bud', 'Best lessons are learnt through bitter experiences' and 'magical in the mundane' (art from folk floor drawings).",["journey","learning","inner"],"IE p. 12 (R Siva Kumar)",["2026A3","2025A4","2023A4"]),
("Story","Lula's comeback at 80","Brazil's Lula finished second in the first round, then won the runoff. No candidate had done that since direct elections returned in 1989. He was re-elected for a record fourth term at 80, after mobilising young voters, though he does not own a mobile phone.","For resilience and 'the cost of being wrong is less than the cost of doing nothing'. Also for leadership and 'leader follows the followers' (youth mobilisation).",["journey","power"],"IE p. 14, 16",["2024B4","2026B4"]),
("Example","Nepal and the first loss-and-damage grant","Nepal accounts for barely 0.1% of global emissions, yet floods triggered by an ice-and-rock avalanche claimed more than 1,400 lives and destroyed at least 10% of its hydropower facilities. Developed countries agreed to the grant only after insisting it be treated as an exception, not a precedent.","Our suggested link: a contemporary illustration of 'When two elephants fight, it is the grass that gets trampled', and of justice versus charity (compensation, not aid).",["power","justice","nature"],"IE p. 12 editorial",["2026A4","2023B3"]),
("Quote","Privy Council, 1872","'The difficulties of a litigant in India begin when he has obtained a Decree', a line still true 150 years later, according to the Ideas-page article on slow civil courts.","For 'History repeats itself' and for irony ('Oxymorons reflect the ironies of life'): winning is the start of the struggle.",["history","justice","inner"],"IE p. 13 (Abhinav Chandrachud)",["2021B3","2026A1"]),
("Book","The Godfather (Mario Puzo, 1969)","The undertaker Bonasera turns to the mafia don when the courts fail him. The author uses it to explain why Indian litigants turn to police 'justice' when civil courts take decades.","For 'A society that has more justice needs less charity' (or less coercion), and for institutions and the rule of law.",["justice","power"],"IE p. 13",["2023B3"]),
("Example","When a verdict becomes 'one opinion among many'","In the Ankita Bhandari case the courts convicted all three accused within about three years. Yet viral, disputed audio clips revived a cover-up narrative. A senior police officer writes that evidence must survive cross-examination while social media runs on plausibility and outrage.","For 'Truth knows no colour', 'The doubter is a true man of science' (healthy versus cynical doubt), and social media topics.",["reason","tech","ethics"],"IE p. 13 (Abhinav Kumar)",["2025A1","2024A4","2024B1"]),
("Example","Dissent inside the Election Commission","Two Election Commissioners recorded 14 written notes of objection in 10 months. Several safeguards they proposed, such as an expert committee to check the software, were later adopted by the Commission.","For 'Thinking is like a game; it does not begin unless there is an opposite team': recorded dissent improves institutional decisions. Also for 'Shelving hard decisions is the least ethical course'.",["reason","ethics","power"],"IE p. 1-2",["2023A1","2026B3"]),
("Example","Lycurgus Cup and Roman concrete: the science in 'magic'","A 1,600-year-old Roman cup glows red in normal light and green when lit from behind. Scientists have only now reverse-engineered it. Roman concrete is still not fully recreated, and Indus Valley drainage still has lessons for cities. The editorial argues that the past's real magic is useful achievement, not myths of flying chariots.","For 'magical in the mundane', 'The doubter is a true man of science' (versus pseudo-history) and 'Culture is what we are, civilisation is what we have'.",["learning","reason","history"],"IE p. 12 editorial",["2023A4","2024A4","2020B1"]),
("Quote","Arthur C Clarke's Third Law","'Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic.'","Intro or turn for technology essays, and for 'Thought finds a world and creates one also'.",["tech","learning"],"IE p. 12 editorial",["2025A3","2020B4"]),
("Example","Glut by day, shortage by night","India's power sector has moved from chronic deficits to a solar surplus in daylight and shortages after dark, so it wastes clean power while scrambling at night. The fix, storage, is slow and needs investment before the crisis.","A modern paradox for 'Oxymorons reflect the ironies of life', and a policy example for 'The time to repair the roof is when the sun is shining'.",["inner","ethics","nature"],"IE p. 12 (Somit Dasgupta)",["2026A1","2022B1"]),
("Story","The farmer who copied a friend's strawberries","In Manauli, Sonipat, Arun Chauhan switched from paddy to sweet corn after visiting a friend's strawberry farm. His village moved 70% of its land to sweet corn and vegetables, formed an FPO and now sells directly to e-commerce firms.","For 'The cost of being wrong is less than the cost of doing nothing', 'Ideas with large consequences are simple' and 'A ship in harbour is safe'.",["journey","learning","ethics"],"IE p. 4",["2024B4","2024B3","2022A4"]),
("Story","Two fathers behind two India cricketers","Behind Tilak Varma and Nitish Kumar Reddy, who won a T20I for India in New Zealand, stand two fathers who had hard years. Nitish's father, Mutyala Reddy, left a stable job at Hindustan Zinc Limited to support his son's practice in Visakhapatnam. Tilak's father, Nagaraju, an electrician, spent sleepless nights making ends meet so that his son could concentrate on cricket.","For the role of parents and family in shaping a child's future: sacrifice made quietly, years before any success is visible. Fits 'Hand that rocks the cradle rules the world' (the family as the first institution).",["ethics","inner"],"IE p. 17",["2021B1"]),
("Story","Angkrish Raghuvanshi's maiden century","Out for 92 on debut three years ago, then dropped, the 22-year-old admitted doubting whether he would ever score a first-class hundred. He finally made 114 for Mumbai.","For 'Best lessons are learnt through bitter experiences' and 'Ships sink because of water that gets into them' (self-doubt as the real threat).",["journey","inner"],"IE p. 17",["2025A4","2020A3"]),
("Story","Harmanpreet Singh's homecoming","The India hockey captain once trained on a tiny astro-turf patch behind the goal at Jalandhar's Surjit Singh stadium. He played there in an India jersey for the first time when international hockey returned to Punjab after 17 years. He said playing there felt like returning to his mother's lap.","For 'The years teach much which the days never know', gratitude and roots, and the journey over the destination.",["journey","inner"],"IE p. 18",["2025B2","2025B3"]),
("Data","FIDE's first survey of women in chess","76% of 672 female respondents from 127 federations reported discrimination or harassment. 44% considered stepping away from chess, and 54% never had a female role model or mentor. India was the largest contributor, with 69 respondents.","Fresh data for gender-justice essays ('Patriarchy is the least noticed yet most significant structure...'). The lack of mentors shows 'restrictions' at work.",["justice"],"IE p. 17",["2020B3","2023B1"]),
("Example","Make the world want India, and need India","Radha Goenka argues for building cultural power that makes the world want India, and economic capability that makes the world need India. Her examples: Taiwan's TSMC makes nearly three-quarters of contract-manufactured chips, and France's tourism earns 9.1% of GDP.","For 'The empires of the future will be the empires of the mind' and for soft power ('subdue the enemy without fighting').",["power","learning"],"IE p. 13 (Radha Goenka)",["2024A2","2025A2"]),
("Quote","Voting is a civic sacrament","Theodore Hesburgh, quoted as the day's 'Wordly Wise'.","A conclusion line for democracy, citizenship and 'leader follows the followers' essays.",["ethics","power"],"IE p. 12",["2026B4"]),
("Phrase","From 'use and throw' to 'use, return and recover'","Delhi's planned bottle-deposit scheme. Uttarakhand's version raised bottle return rates from about 20% to nearly 90% with a refundable Rs 10 deposit.","For nature and ethics essays: small nudges, big behaviour change ('Ideas with large consequences are simple').",["nature","ethics"],"IE p. 11",["2024B3","2026B1"]),
("Example","A century-old antivenom gets an upgrade","Antivenom treatment had barely changed in a century, while India loses an estimated 50,000 people a year to snakebite. IISc and Danish scientists built an antibody cocktail that saved mice even 30 minutes after cobra venom was injected.","For 'What is research, but a blind date with knowledge' and 'A well-educated mind will always have more questions than answers'.",["reason","learning"],"IE p. 16",["2021B2","2026B2"]),
("Example","Ageing with dignity: Keralam's elder-rights law","Keralam's new law gives senior citizens the right to age at home, recognises living wills, and regulates care homes and caregivers. The Chief Minister called it a realisation that 'our world is changing'.","For 'Life is a long journey between human being and being humane', 'The years teach much...' and 'Hand that rocks the cradle' (care work).",["journey","justice","inner"],"IE p. 8",["2020A1","2025B2"]),
]
